{
	title: 'Portable plugin imports and optional file-local hooks',
	spec: 'Plugins retain full in-process authority: before/after/around/set remain unrestricted. Hal makes file-local behavior convenient rather than enforcing project scope. Runtime imports hal/<path> resolve to the running Hal source tree regardless of plugin location; TypeScript resolves the same names. Plugins can remain single portable TS files and sync through existing reviewed, hash-checked history without embedding machine paths. Filesystem scope is an optional path containment predicate, independent of session cwd. EDIT offers an asynchronous text transform point before final diff/size validation and before any write; transform failures leave the batch unwritten, and diffs, verification and rollback use the transformed bytes. File-change observation offers an awaitable notification after recorded EDIT and declared Bash changes, with paths and existing ToolContext; no global filesystem crawler or claims to observe arbitrary external edits. Plugin failures remove hooks but preserve source bytes and filename; unchanged failed bytes are not retried on repeated events, edited bytes retry, deletion removes the error entry. Failure reporting remains complete. Preserve portable metadata for setup failures and stop load races on disappeared files from becoming unhandled rejections. Existing sync must demonstrate copying a real portable plugin and loading it on the destination. Document author guidance for cleanup, file-local effects, process roles and portable dependencies. Project auto-discovery awaits the separate trust design; no sandbox, registry or broad lifecycle framework.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Keep the existing hook composition, cleanup and reviewed sync instead of adding a new event registry, scoped loader or AsyncLocalStorage. Optional pluginFiles hooks are concrete module functions. EDIT transforms run before diffing, backups and writing, never during rollback. Changed observers run after recording and releasing file locks, so follow-up tool work cannot deadlock on the original operation. Restored invariant: a runtime load failure must not mutate portable source or masquerade as a synced deletion; leave failed bytes in place and deduplicate watcher events. The old .ts.broken rename rule is replaced. hal/*.ts resolves via one lazy Bun resolver, with matching TypeScript paths; imports refer to actual module singletons, not forwarding wrappers. No new dependencies, polling or per-session state.',
		'Verified with the full ./test suite: 1686 pass, 2 existing platform skips, 0 fail, plus clean full TypeScript and lint. A sync integration test transfers real plugin bytes using the existing comparison/apply protocol, loads them in a fresh Bun process outside the Hal tree, and verifies hal imports resolve the destination home. Other checks cover file-local transforms, exact resulting output, batch failure, creation/deletion observers, Bash observer errors after unlock, failed portable setup metadata and disappeared files. The first full-suite invocation hit the tool command time limit at 20 seconds; the completed run took 60.39 seconds with no test timeout changes. No live remote host was modified. Runtime overhead is one lazy resolver per process, one transform call per changed EDIT file, and one observer call per nonempty recorded change batch; no additional watchers or accumulating state.'
